# Validate pasted model responses transactionally

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical notes describe current implementation evidence and gaps. The
finite acceptance below does not imply those gaps were implemented.

Parse the complete response into a candidate notebook, show all differences and
errors, and change the accepted session only after explicit acceptance.

## Finite untrusted-response review and atomic acceptance contract

**Inputs:** one complete pasted model-response text, the current accepted
revision, backend parser/semantic schema identity and caller intent to inspect
or accept a proposed notebook. **Outputs:** a bounded candidate with an
ordered semantic diff and complete diagnostics, a refusal, or one newly
accepted revision only after a separate explicit user acceptance. Pasted
bytes and untrusted claims cannot supply file/device authority.

**Review sequence:** preserve raw input as inert text, decode the complete
versioned envelope with strict size/schema checks, validate references and
semantic ownership against one base revision, then build a candidate without
mutating the current notebook. Show the old and candidate values by stable
semantic IDs plus all blocking and unresolved diagnostics. Only an explicit
Accept action bound to the same base revision may submit one atomic backend
transaction; Cancel leaves all accepted semantic state unchanged.

**Worked example:** revision `r20` contains title identity `t1` with text
`Chapter 1`. A complete model response proposes `Chapter 1: Cells` for
the same semantic owner; the review shows one text diff `t1`, unchanged
other objects and a complete nonblocking diagnostic set. Accepting exactly
this reviewed proposal against `r20` creates `r21` and an explicit receipt;
rejecting it instead preserves `r20` and the raw response for revision.

**Refusal example:** a payload names nonexistent figure `f99`, includes a
filesystem export path as an instruction, or is too large for the admitted
parser. No candidate may be accepted and the existing revision stays intact.
If the session advances to `r21` during review, an earlier `r20` proposal is
`StaleBase`, even if it was otherwise valid; the user must review a fresh diff.

Incomplete diagnostic evidence blocks acceptance rather than treating omitted
error detail as success. Semantic commands in the raw text follow the same
backend boundary, never a browser-side best-effort patch.

**Assumptions and limits:** the backend has partial candidate validation,
accepted revision ownership and atomic semantic transaction primitives.
Browser paste-to-candidate parsing, complete versioned wire response formats,
review UI, complete diagnostics and explicit acceptance transport remain open.
This task is a theoretical review design, not a claim that arbitrary model
output is currently safe or usable.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** theoretical for full browser-assisted response review and commit;
  partial practical backend candidate/transaction primitives, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** one accepted/rejected title diff, explicit human acceptance,
  stale-base and invalid-payload refusal, atomic notebook invariant.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** complete pasted response parser,
  candidate UI, full model format, or browser-to-backend acceptance workflow.
